== Homotopy
<homotopy>
#statement("Definition")[
$pi_n\(X\):=\[S^n\,X\]$ are the homotopy groups, the relative homotopy groups $pi_n\(X\,A\)$ are defined to be all homotopy classes of maps $\(I^n\,partial I^n\)arrow.r\(X\,A\)$ or equivalently all homotopy classes of maps $\(S^n\,s_0\)arrow.r\(X\,A\)$, in particular, if $A = { x_0 }$, we have $pi_n\(X\,x_0\):= chevron.l S^n\,X chevron.r$ with basepoints $x_0\,s_0$, furthermore, we also define $pi_n\(X\,A\,x_0\)$ to be all homotopy classes of maps $\(I^n\,partial I^n\,J^(n - 1)\)arrow.r\(X\,A\,x_0\)$ or equivalently all homotopy classes of maps $\(D^n\,S^(n - 1)\,s_0\)arrow.r\(X\,A\,x_0\)$, here $J^(n - 1) = partial I^n - I^(n - 1)$

$pi_1\(X\,x_0\)$ is called the fundamental group, note that $pi_n\(X\,x\,x\)= pi_n\(X\,x\)$

]
#statement("Definition")[
All homotopy classes of paths form the #emph[fundamental groupoid]#index-entry("Fundamental groupoid") $Pi_1\(X\)$ of $X$, suppose $A subset.eq X$, we can also define $Pi_1\(X\,A\)$ to be the full subcategory with objects $x in A$ and morphisms $"Hom"\(x\,y\)\,x\,y in A$, $Pi_1\(X\,x\)= pi_1\(X\,x\)$, $Pi_1\(X\)$ is a connected category if $X$ is path-connected since there is a morphism connecting any two objects, thus $pi_1\(X\,x\)$ is a skeleton of $Pi_1\(X\)$, $pi_1\(X\,x\)arrow.r.hook Pi_1\(X\)$ is an equivalence of categories

]
#statement("Proposition")[
$pi_n\(X\,x_0\)$ are groups, abelian if $n > 1$

]
#statement("Theorem", title: [Van Kampen's theorem])[
Suppose $X = union.big_alpha A_alpha$, interiors of $A_alpha$ cover $X$, where $X\,A_alpha\,A_alpha inter A_beta$ are path connected and $x_0 in A_alpha$, then the map induced by inclusion $*_alpha pi_1\(A_alpha\,x_0\)arrow.r pi_1\(X\,x_0\)$ is surjective. Moreover, if $A_alpha inter A_beta inter A_gamma$ are path connected, then then kernel is generated by $i_alpha\(w\)i_beta\(w\)^(- 1)\,w in pi_1\(A_alpha inter A_beta\,x_0\)$, where $i_alpha : A_alpha arrow.r X$ are the inclusions

]#label("Van Kampen's theorem")
#proof(title: "Proof")[
Since $A_alpha inter A_beta$ are path connected, we can cut a loop in $X$ into pieces such that each intermediate point is in $A_alpha inter A_beta$ for some $alpha\,beta$, thus the map is surjective

